<section class="shell band">
  <h1>Getting licensed as an LPC in Texas</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>Full LPC licensure in Texas requires a qualifying graduate degree, two exams, an LPC
    Associate license, and then <strong>3,000 clock-hours</strong> of supervised experience
    under a Council-approved supervisor. At least <strong>1,500</strong> of those hours must be
    direct client counseling contact, and the 3,000 cannot be completed in fewer than
    <strong>18 months</strong>. While you are accruing them you must receive at least
    <strong>four hours of supervision a month</strong>, with no more than half of your total
    supervision hours in a group. Your LPC Associate license expires 60 months from issuance
    and cannot be renewed.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="source">Rules cited below are from 22 Texas Administrative Code, Chapter 681,
  administered by the <a href="https://bhec.texas.gov">Texas Behavioral Health Executive
  Council</a>. Current as of September 2026. Rules change, so verify anything you are about to
  rely on against BHEC directly.</p>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>The path, in four stages</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Each stage has to be complete before the next one counts.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it takes</th><th>Rule</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Graduate degree</td>
            <td>60 semester hours for programs begun on or after 1 August 2017, 48 before that,
            including a supervised practicum of at least 300 clock-hours with at least 100
            hours of direct client counseling, carrying academic credit</td>
            <td>§681.82, §681.83</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Exams</td>
            <td>The NCE or the NCMHCE, plus the Texas Jurisprudence Exam. Your jurisprudence
            certificate must be dated no more than six months before your application is
            received</td>
            <td>§681.72</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>LPC Associate license</td>
            <td>Apply and be licensed before you begin. Hours worked while unlicensed do not
            count, in any setting. The license expires 60 months from issuance and cannot be
            renewed</td>
            <td>§681.72, §681.140</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Supervised experience</td>
            <td>3,000 clock-hours, at least 1,500 of them direct client counseling contact,
            over no fewer than 18 months, under a Council-approved supervisor</td>
            <td>§681.92</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>The hours, and how long they actually take</h2>
  <p>The 3,000 is rarely the binding constraint. The 1,500 direct hours are, because direct
  hours accrue only while you are in session with a client. Everything else, notes, admin,
  training, staffing, counts toward the other 1,500.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Time to reach 1,500 direct hours, before holidays, no-shows and ramp-up.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Direct client hours a week</th><th>Weeks to 1,500</th><th>Roughly</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>25</td><td>60</td><td>18 months, held to the floor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20</td><td>75</td><td>18 months, held to the floor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>15</td><td>100</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12</td><td>125</td><td>2 years 5 months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>188</td><td>3 years 7 months</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Two things fall out of that table. Above roughly twenty direct hours a week, the 18-month
  floor decides your timeline, not your caseload, so pushing harder buys you nothing. Below
  about twelve, the 60-month expiry on your Associate license starts to become the real
  deadline, and cancellations and slow referral months eat the margin faster than most
  associates expect.</p>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>The supervision rules associates get wrong</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Four hours a month is a minimum, not a target.</strong> You must receive at
      least four hours of supervision in every month you are counseling, unless your
      supervisor approves an extended leave of a month or more in writing.</li>
      <li><strong>Individual means up to two associates.</strong> BHEC defines individual
      supervision as a supervisor with one or two associates. Three or more is group.</li>
      <li><strong>No more than half your total hours can be group.</strong> Over the whole
      period, group supervision cannot exceed 50% of your supervision hours. This is why a
      structure of four individual hours plus one group hour a month works cleanly: group is a
      fifth of the total.</li>
      <li><strong>Two supervisors, maximum.</strong> You may have up to two LPC supervisors on
      file at a time, splitting hours by mutual agreement.</li>
      <li><strong>Paperwork has deadlines.</strong> The Supervisory Agreement Form goes in
      before supervision starts. The Supervised Experience Documentation Form is due within 30
      days after supervision ends.</li>
      <li><strong>Miss the 60 months and you start over.</strong> If the required hours are not
      complete when your Associate license expires, you reapply and meet whatever requirements
      are in effect at that time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/for-therapists/lpc-supervision/">How supervision with Reem is structured</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Choosing a supervisor</h2>
  <p>You will spend eighteen months to three years with this person, during the period that
  shapes how you practice for the rest of your career. Interview them.</p>
  <h3>Worth asking</h3>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>How many associates are you supervising right now, and what is your cap?</li>
    <li>What is the fee, what does it include, and does it change if my caseload changes?</li>
    <li>Is the fee monthly or hourly, and what happens in a month when I only need two hours?</li>
    <li>How is group supervision structured, and how many people are in it?</li>
    <li>Are you reachable between sessions when something urgent comes up?</li>
    <li>What are your clinical models, and are you willing to supervise work outside them?</li>
    <li>Will you help me build a practice, or only sign hours?</li>
    <li>How do you give corrective feedback, and can you give me an example?</li>
    <li>Who employs me, and does that create a conflict with who supervises me?</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Reasons to walk away</h3>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>No written supervision agreement.</li>
    <li>The Supervisory Agreement Form is not filed before you start seeing clients.</li>
    <li>Hours are not documented contemporaneously, or records are treated as the supervisor's
    property.</li>
    <li>Sessions get canceled and not rescheduled, leaving you short for the month.</li>
    <li>You cannot get a straight answer about fees.</li>
    <li>Feedback is only reassurance. If nothing you do is ever questioned, you are paying for
    a signature.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>What supervision costs</h2>
    <p>Fee structures vary more than the headline number suggests. Some supervisors charge per
    hour, some charge a flat monthly rate covering the full four hours, and some charge less per
    hour but exclude group. Agencies sometimes cover supervision entirely, sometimes in exchange
    for a lower fee split, which is a real cost even when it is not an invoice.</p>
    <p>Compare on the monthly total for four compliant hours, not on the hourly rate. Then ask
    what else is included: EMDR or modality consultation, practice-building help, a placement
    site, retreats, access between sessions.</p>
    <p>For reference, supervision with Reem is $425 a month covering four individual hours and
    one group hour, or $500 a month on a three-month contract for associates inside an agency.
    EMDR consultation is included at no cost while you are her associate.</p>
