<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Renewing your Texas LPC license</h2>
  <p>Renewal is where most licensed counselors interact with the rules, and it is separate from
  everything above: the 3,000 hours get you licensed once, the renewal cycle keeps you licensed
  for the rest of your career. What BHEC requires each renewal period is continuing education,
  the jurisprudence exam, and the fee.</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <div><dt>Continuing education</dt><dd>24 hours in each renewal period</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Ethics inside that 24</dt><dd>At least 6 hours</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Cultural diversity and competency</dt><dd>At least 3 hours, inside the 24</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Approved providers</dt><dd>Since 1 January 2024, at least half of the 24 hours must come from a provider listed in §681.140(f)</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Human trafficking prevention</dt><dd>An HHSC-approved course</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Jurisprudence exam</dt><dd>Passed each renewal period, and it counts as one hour of ethics credit</dd></div>
    <div><dt>LPC Supervisors</dt><dd>A further 6 hours of supervision-specific continuing education per renewal period, counted inside the 24</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Fees, dates and late renewal</dt><dd>Set by BHEC and not reproduced here. Check your renewal notice and the BHEC renewal page before your expiry date.</dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p>Two things catch people out. The first is the approved-provider rule: hours from an
  unlisted provider still count toward professional development but not toward your required
  half, so check the provider before you pay, not after. The second is that supervision CE for
  an LPC-S is not additional to the 24, it sits inside it, which means a supervisor has less
  free choice about the other hours than they might assume.</p>
  <p>This page does not list renewal fees or late-renewal windows, because those are set by
  BHEC and change more often than the rule text does. Take them from your renewal notice or
  from BHEC directly rather than from any third-party site, this one included.</p>
  <p><a href="/for-therapists/retreats-training/">Ethics CEUs and training with Reem</a></p>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Becoming a supervisor</h2>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>Becoming an LPC-S</h3>
      <p>Supervisor status requires an unrestricted LPC license in good standing for at least
      five years, plus either a 40-clock-hour supervision course meeting §681.147 or a
      for-credit doctoral-level course in supervision. Once approved, holding it requires 6
      hours of supervision-specific continuing education per renewal period, which count inside
      the 24.</p>
      <p>BHEC's current guidance says the supervision course does not expire once completed,
      while the rule text has historically referenced recency windows. If your certificate is
      old, confirm with BHEC before you apply.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>What it lets you do</h3>
      <p>An approved LPC-S can supervise LPC Associates toward their own 3,000 hours, which is
      the arrangement this whole guide describes from the other side. It is also the credential
      that makes a practice's supervision revenue possible, which is why the five-year
      unrestricted-license requirement exists.</p>
      <p>Supervisory status is maintained on the same renewal cycle as the license itself, with
      the 6 supervision CE hours counted inside the 24.</p>
      <p><a href="/for-therapists/lpc-supervision/">Supervision with Reem, an LPC-S since
      2009</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Texas LPC licensure questions</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>How many supervised hours do you need for an LPC in Texas?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>3,000 clock-hours of supervised experience under a Council-approved supervisor, of
      which at least 1,500 must be direct client counseling contact. Only actual time spent
      counseling counts as direct. The 3,000 cannot be completed in fewer than 18 months, per
      22 TAC §681.92.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How many hours of supervision does an LPC Associate need each month?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>A minimum of four hours a month while you are counseling. Individual supervision means
      a supervisor with one or two associates; group means three or more. No more than 50% of
      your total supervision hours may be group. You can have up to two LPC supervisors on file
      at once.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How long does it take to become an LPC in Texas?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Eighteen months at the absolute fastest after you are licensed as an Associate,
      because that floor applies regardless of how quickly you accrue hours. At fifteen direct
      client hours a week it takes about two years; at eight a week, closer to three and a half.
      Add the two to three years of graduate study before that.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does my LPC Associate license expire?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. It expires 60 months from the date of issuance and cannot be renewed. If you have
      not completed the required supervised experience in that window, you must reapply for
      licensure and meet the requirements in effect at the time of reapplication.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do hours count if I worked before getting my Associate license?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Hours obtained by an unlicensed person do not count toward the supervised
      experience requirement, in any setting. Get licensed first.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I do supervision online in Texas?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. Texas rules set the amount and structure of supervision rather than requiring it
      to be in person, and online supervision is common across the state. Reem Glasco, LPC-S,
      supervises online only, which means associates anywhere in Texas can work with her.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What qualifies someone to supervise LPC Associates in Texas?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>An unrestricted Texas LPC license in good standing for at least five years, plus a
      40-clock-hour supervision course meeting 22 TAC §681.147 or a for-credit doctoral-level
      course in supervision, and approval by BHEC as an LPC-S. Verify any supervisor's status on
      the BHEC license lookup before signing an agreement.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What do you need to renew a Texas LPC license?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>24 hours of continuing education in each renewal period, including at least 6 hours of
      ethics and 3 hours of cultural diversity and competency, an HHSC-approved human
      trafficking prevention course, and a pass on the jurisprudence exam, which itself counts
      as one hour of ethics credit. Since 1 January 2024, at least half of the 24 hours must
      come from a provider listed in 22 TAC §681.140(f). Fees and dates come from BHEC.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How many CE hours does a Texas LPC Supervisor need?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>The same 24 as any LPC, with 6 of them in supervision-specific continuing education to
      maintain supervisory status. Those 6 sit inside the 24 rather than on top of it, so an
      LPC-S has fewer discretionary hours than a licensee without the credential.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do continuing education hours from any provider count?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Not toward the whole requirement. Since 1 January 2024 at least half of the 24 hours
      must come from a provider listed in §681.140(f). Hours from an unlisted provider can
      still be worth taking, but they do not fill that half, so confirm the provider's status
      before booking rather than after.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

